{
  "requirement_items": [
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.9",
      "label": null,
      "text": "Safety equipment including local e-stops and any sensors and wiring that interface with the Observatory Safety System will be provided.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.10",
      "label": null,
      "text": "The Test Instruments system decomposition element is defined as follows:",
      "id": "REQ-1-OAD-0164"
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.10",
      "label": null,
      "text": "The Optical Test Instruments include the prime focus camera with all of its supports, cables, controls, and interfaces and the global metrology system (GMS), which consists of telescope mounted surveying instruments in insulated, light-tight enclosures, along with the associated controls and cabling.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.10",
      "label": null,
      "text": "It also includes all electronics and software required to integrate and utilize the Prime Focus Camera (PFC) and the Global Metrology System (GMS).",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.10",
      "label": null,
      "text": "The PFC will be used to verify that the initial 120 segments have been installed correctly and to conduct early pointing and tracking tests.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.10",
      "label": null,
      "text": "The TINS will provide the electronic, software, and user interfaces to support these measurements and tests.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.10",
      "label": null,
      "text": "Use of the PFC is not expected past the installation of the first 120 segments.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.10",
      "label": null,
      "text": "During operations, the GMS will be used to measure the relative positions of the M1, M2, M3, instruments and reference features on the telescope structure and the fixed base.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.10",
      "label": null,
      "text": "This data will be used to update the rigid body LUTs for M2 and M3 as well as the mount pointing model and to verify alignment in the event that components are replaced or adjusted.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.10",
      "label": null,
      "text": "The TINS will provide the electronics and software required to interface the GMS with the Telescope Control System and other observatory sub-systems.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.10",
      "label": null,
      "text": "It will be possible to utilize GMS measurements in manual and fully automated modes.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.10",
      "label": null,
      "text": "The GMS will include an expert user GUI.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.11",
      "label": null,
      "text": "The Optics Handling system decomposition element is defined as follows:",
      "id": "REQ-1-OAD-0167"
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.11",
      "label": null,
      "text": "The Optical Handling Equipment is used to install, remove and transport the optical assemblies of the telescope.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.11",
      "label": null,
      "text": "It includes all of the lifting and handling fixtures/ frames and transportation carts for M1, M2 and M3 along with the associated lifting accessories, including HydraSets, slings, and connecting hardware.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.11",
      "label": null,
      "text": "For the M2 and M3, separate lifting fixtures are required for the entire assembly and for the mirror alone.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.11",
      "label": null,
      "text": "The Optical Handling Equipment also includes the storage racks for the spare segments.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.11",
      "label": null,
      "text": "It does not include the cranes which are included in the Enclosure or the M1 segment handling system which is included in the Telescope Structure.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.11",
      "label": null,
      "text": "It does not include the crane attachments required for in-situ optics cleaning, which are included in the Optical Cleaning Systems.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.11",
      "label": null,
      "text": "It does not include the segment jacks, which are included in the M1 Optics System.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.12",
      "label": null,
      "text": "The Alignment and Phasing system decomposition element is defined as follows:",
      "id": "REQ-1-OAD-0170"
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.12",
      "label": null,
      "text": "The Alignment and Phasing System (APS) is responsible for the rigid body alignment of the M1, M2 and M3, as well as adjusting the surface figure degrees of freedom for the M1.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.12",
      "label": null,
      "text": "As part of the alignment process APS will have the capability to phase the 492 M1 segments.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.12",
      "label": null,
      "text": "APS will use starlight to measure the wavefront errors and then will determine the appropriate corrections to align the optics.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.12",
      "label": null,
      "text": "The APS will align the telescope at various elevation angles and then from the set points for the M1, M2 and M3 control systems, lookup tables will be generated to correct for gravity-induced deformations.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.12",
      "label": null,
      "text": "In a similar fashion, data will be collected at various temperatures over time and lookup tables will be built as a function of temperature as well.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.12",
      "label": null,
      "text": "APS is not responsible for the generation of the LUTs.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.12",
      "label": null,
      "text": "APS includes all the necessary hardware, software, and interfaces (to the TCS; and M1, M2, and M3 control systems) required to accomplish the alignment tasks defined above.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.12",
      "label": null,
      "text": "APS will have an acquisition camera with a 1 to 2 arcminute field of view which can be used for telescope pointing, acquisition, and tracking tests.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.12",
      "label": null,
      "text": "APS will also provide an optical port where a guider camera and a low order wavefront sensor can be placed in order to test its performance and to validate the active optics control algorithms.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.12",
      "label": null,
      "text": "APS will provide an expert user GUI.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.13",
      "label": null,
      "text": "The Telescope Control system decomposition element is defined as follows:",
      "id": "REQ-1-OAD-0173"
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.13",
      "label": null,
      "text": "The TCS is responsible for the coordination and control of the various subsystems that comprise the telescope system.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.13",
      "label": null,
      "text": "The TCS primarily consists of software and the associated off the shelf computer hardware necessary to perform the following functions.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.13",
      "label": null,
      "text": "The TCS consists of a Sequencer and Status/Alarm Monitor, a Pointing Kernel, a Corrections Module, and several adaptors.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.13",
      "label": null,
      "text": "The Sequencer and the Status/Alarm Monitor controls and coordinates the telescope systems based on commands received from the Observatory Control System (OCS) and expert user interfaces.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.13",
      "label": null,
      "text": "The TCS Sequencer and Status/Alarm Monitor provides high level control of the mount, M1, M2, M3, and the enclosure (cap, base, shutter, vents).",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.13",
      "label": null,
      "text": "The enclosure vents will be controllable individually or via pre-set configurations; the design will provide the hooks enabling future automated control of vent configurations based on environmental conditions",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.13",
      "label": null,
      "text": "The TCS pointing kernel converts target positions (right ascension and declination) into pointing and tracking demands in the appropriate coordinate system for use by the telescope mount; instrument and AO WFS probes, atmospheric dispersion correctors, rotators; and the enclosure cap and base.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.13",
      "label": null,
      "text": "In seeing limited operation, the correction module receives and processes focus, tip/tilt, coma and low radial order corrections from an instrument WFS that have been reconstructed and rotated into telescope mount, M1, and M2 coordinates.",
      "id": null
    }
  ],
